00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.340 align:start position:0%
 
if you've been learning a language for  some time do you happen to ask yourself  questions such as what level am I at am  I actually making progress when am I  gonna reach the next level am i  improving my grammar pronunciation or  fluency in that target language hi my  name is Luca from Lucca lepercahn and  today we're going to talk about how to  measure progress we're learning a  foreign language there is really nothing  wrong with asking yourself where you  stand and where you're going I did that  all the time back in the day when I was  at school when I was fifteen I can  remember as clear as day that I was  jotting down all my language goals and I  was fantasizing about learning tons of  languages and I was writing down the  level at which I thought I was and where  I wanted to go with time and experience  I've just realized that while setting up  goals is important the most important  and efficient way to tackle how to  measure foreign languages is actually to  set up a system which will allow you to  learn in the long term and that is  developing of the right habits  how do I build a system the people who  follow my blog have noticed that I've  recently published some log books where  I keep meticulously I would say track of  my learning process I think that having  a log book is a great idea you can keep  track of your progress but how do you  measure progress well one thing that  I've recently added and I think it's  making a difference is actually to add  some self-reflection exercises after  writing what I did and how long it took  me I normally spend two or three minutes  even 10 minutes when it's necessary  shutting down the things I did and asked  you myself whether the things I'm doing  are actually good for me and how I can  refine them in fact the most important  goal is not only to keep track of my  progress but actually to refine the  system through which I am actually  learning so I ask things such as am I  using the right resource is this  resource nice and good and  comprehensible and intelligible and  interesting for me at this level what  else can I add how can I organize my  time and these bits and pieces of  information are actually added up and  they're helping me refine the system  better and better refining the system  means to be able to learn in the long  term the language learning is always and  always will be a marathon and if you  want to be successful the most important  thing you want to do is actually stick  to it over the long haul and with time  I've developed a system which allows me  to learn not only one language but two  or even three at the same time  in language running there are things you  can more or less measure for example the  amount of words that you know you can  save them you can look back at them and  say okay this is more or less the list  of words that I know but unfortunately  language learning is not just a corpus  of knowledge it's also a skill that you  develop and it entails a number of sub  skills in and of itself and within  itself for example being able to speak  being able to write being able to read  and so on and so forth and unfortunately  it is much more difficult to not only to  keep track of this but also to measure  whether you're actually making progress  or not in his popular book peek secrets  from the new science of expertise the  author Anders Ericsson makes the case  that experts in any field actually  operate within a consistent framework  that they built and this framework is  based upon consistent mental  representations that these experts  develop with time it's a really really  interesting book and I recommend you  read it so how do I operate within the  language learning framework what are my  mental representations I divide language  running mainly in three areas beginner  intermediate and advanced actually I  define my level according to the  resources that I use the what and how I  use them  the how let me give you an example if  I'm at the very early stage of language  learning that is I'm a beginner I always  use a book like a steam eel or teach  yourself or another resource and I apply  my bidirectional method to it so I  define the what I know the how and for  me I am at this beginner stage so after  a Finnish Hospital I did not ask myself  whether I have reached an intermediate  level or not for me it's actually  automatic and once I get into the  intermediate realm intermediate ground  whatever you want to call it I just  changed the what that is the resource or  resources and I also changed the how a  lot of people think that my  bi-directional translation is a complete  method to learn a language but the  reality is that mine is a combination of  methods which evolved with time so what  do I do when I have reached an  intermediate level  again I changed the what and I changed  the how most often than not I find some  resources on the internet podcasts with  scripts and audio that I can keep  working on but I do not use my  bi-directional translation anymore I do  more intensive listening and reading and  jotting down vocabulary and also I start  talking with some tutors online or with  some friends and in the advanced phase I  do something different such as starting  watching movies or starting absorbing  more authentic content the key point  here is that you should not ask yourself  whether you are beginner intermediate or  advanced I think that it's much more  important to focus on building a  consistent system that works for you a  kind of mental framework where you  constantly know where you are and if you  do that for one language then you can  simply replicate it for any other  language it really helps you not only  figuring out where you are but also  motivates you in the long run because  you know where you're going  learning a language is a little bit like  climbing a mountain or setting upon a  very long journey it takes time it's an  endless journey actually because we  never stop learning so instead of  focusing whether you're constantly  making progress because if you're  walking or you're climbing or you're  running you are getting to your  destination or getting closer and closer  I think you should focus on the small  victories that are along the journey let  me give you an example about what I do  when I happen to have these Eureka  moments or celebrate victories when it  comes to reading I really get excited  when for example I stumble upon a  Wikipedia article about something I  really like and I understand the gist of  it well that's great or when I listen to  something podcasts or a movie and I  start understanding bits and pieces of  this I clearly remember when that  happened when I was watching a Swedish  movie I got so excited about that  speaking when I first have a  conversation on skype with someone and I  can get my point across  or when a stumble upon someone I am in a  given country and I happen to have a  conversation with someone that happened  in Hungary in Budapest some three years  ago I started learning Hungarian for  some time and then I started talking to  this granny who could actually  understand me I was so excited about  that these are small Victor's these are  the things you want to focus on because  these small Victor's will push you  through exactly like they will push  climbers to move from one base camp to  the other you reach this thing so there  you have it first focus on the system  instead of goals setting up goals is  fine but I think that refining the  system is so much better because at the  end of the day that's what is going to  allow you to learn in the long term  second develop your own mental  representations of where you stand and  where you're going in my case its  beginner intermediate advanced and each  stage corresponds to a certain kind of  material I use and how I use it  and third celebrate small victories  because small victories do count and add  up in the long run as usual this video  comes with a very lengthy and thorough  article I've put together on my blog if  you liked the content of this video you  can click on the link in the description  box and you will find a ton of other  material resources links and whatnot  thank you very much for listening to me  and I'll talk to you very very soon  [Music]

